<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 45 M5, AC5.1 / D6: A CLIP PLACED ON, DROPPED ON OR MOVED TO A SYSTEM
  LANE IS REFUSED.

  SObject::acceptsClips() has existed since M1 as a predicate WITH NO CONSUMER:
  the testkit asserted it and nothing read it, so the policy it describes was
  enforced nowhere. splacements::placementLaneAt() is the consumer, and this
  case is what says the policy is real rather than declared.

  IT IS A SEPARATE RESOLVER, NOT A WIDENED laneAt(), and that is the design
  decision this case protects. laneAt() is also how a clip is REMOVED, resized,
  split and how a clip's PARENT is found; refusing a system lane there would
  make material that somehow reached one impossible to take off again, and would
  turn every read path into a policy decision. The policy belongs at the
  DESTINATION and nowhere else.

  THE CONVERTED CALLERS ARE ENUMERATED, because D6 asks for the list rather than
  a claim that none were missed:

    add-sample                 saddsampleaction.cpp
    place-clip                 splaceclipaction.cpp
    place-recording            splacerecordingaction.cpp
    duplicate-clip (dest)      sduplicateclipaction.cpp
    add-take                   saddtakeaction.cpp
    insert-midi-clip           smidiclipactions.cpp
    place-midi-recording       smidirecordactions.cpp
    import-midi-file (explicit track)   smidifileactions.cpp
    duplicate-asset-here       sduplicateassethereaction.cpp
    move-clip (dest)           smoveclipaction.cpp
    place-asset                splaceassetaction.cpp

  DELIBERATELY NOT CONVERTED, and each for a reason: remove-clip,
  remove-sample, remove-take and the restore inverses (taking material OFF a
  lane must never be refused); resize-clip and split-clip (edits, not
  placements); unpack-clips (it moves material OUT of a fragment); and
  remove-track / restore-track, whose laneAt() resolves a PARENT rather than a
  destination.

  A REFUSAL MUST LEAVE THE MODEL UNTOUCHED (AC5.5), so every refusal below is
  followed by a count that would move if it had half-applied.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="master_refuses_clips">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="0" filePath="../test_autosaw.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- The predicate itself, which M1 built and nothing read until now. -->
    <assert-system-lane trackPath="$master" acceptsClips="0"/>
    <assert-system-lane trackPath="0" role="none" acceptsClips="1"/>

    <!-- add-sample: the drop path, and the one a user reaches first. -->
    <add-sample trackPath="$master" filePath="../test_autosaw.wav" timePos="0"
                expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- place-clip. -->
    <place-clip trackPath="$master" filePath="../test_autosaw.wav" timePos="0"
                expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- insert-midi-clip: the event half of the same policy. -->
    <insert-midi-clip trackPath="$master" timePos="0" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- move-clip, whose DESTINATION is the master. -->
    <move-clip clip="0,0" destTrack="$master" startTime="0" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- AC5.5: NOTHING HALF-APPLIED. The user track still holds its clip at 0
         (so the move did not take it away), and the master holds no clip 0 at
         all, which the refused assertion is what says. -->
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="$master,0" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- ...and the ordinary lane still accepts everything, so the refusals
         above are the POLICY and not a resolver that stopped working. -->
    <add-sample trackPath="0" filePath="../test_autosaw.wav" timePos="192000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" startTime="192000"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
